spec write-skew: 2 sessions, 20 permutations
# A5B write skew (Berenson et al., SIGMOD 1995 §4.4.4). Two on-call doctors; the
# rule is that at least one must stay on call. Each transaction reads the count of
# on-call doctors, sees 2, and takes ITSELF off call — writing a different node, so
# there is no write-write conflict to detect. Snapshot isolation ALLOWS this and
# the invariant ends up violated. That is the documented price of SI, not a defect;
# this transcript exists so the day GoGraph offers serialisability the change is
# visible as a diff rather than as folklore.

starting permutation: s1r s1w s1c s2r s2w s2c
step s1r: MATCH (d:Doctor) WHERE d.oncall = true RETURN count(d) AS oncall
oncall
------
2     
(1 row)
step s1w: MATCH (d:Doctor {name:'alice'}) SET d.oncall = false RETURN d.name AS who
who    
-------
"alice"
(1 row)
step s1c: COMMIT
step s2r: MATCH (d:Doctor) WHERE d.oncall = true RETURN count(d) AS oncall
oncall
------
2     
(1 row)
step s2w: MATCH (d:Doctor {name:'bob'}) SET d.oncall = false RETURN d.name AS who
who  
-----
"bob"
(1 row)
step s2c: COMMIT

